### Section 5 — Dual-Stream Model Architecture

```
  ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐
  │ Optical  │        │   SAR    │
  │  Image   │        │  Image   │
  └────┬─────┘        └────┬─────┘
       │                    │
  ┌────▼─────┐        ┌────▼─────┐
  │  Shared  │        │  Shared  │
  │   ViT    │ (frozen)│   ViT    │  (same weights)
  └────┬─────┘        └────┬─────┘
       │                    │
  ┌────▼─────┐        ┌────▼─────┐
  │  Shared  │        │  Shared  │
  │ Q-Former │ (frozen)│ Q-Former │  (same weights)
  └────┬─────┘        └────┬─────┘
       │                    │
  [32 tokens]          [32 tokens]
       │                    │
       └────────┬───────────┘
                │
           ┌────▼────┐
           │ CONCAT  │  →  [64 visual tokens]
           └────┬────┘
                │
           ┌────▼────┐
           │ Language │
           │Projection│
           └────┬────┘
                │
           ┌────▼────┐
           │  OPT    │ ← LoRA adapters (trainable)
           │  2.7B   │
           └────┬────┘
                │
           [Answer]
```

Implementation: A `DualStreamBLIP2` wrapper class that:
1. Takes `optical_pixel_values` and `sar_pixel_values` as separate inputs.
2. Runs each through the **shared frozen** `model.vision_model` → `model.qformer`.
3. **Concatenates** the two 32-token Q-Former outputs → 64 tokens.
4. Projects through `model.language_projection`.
5. Prepends to text embeddings and feeds to the **LoRA-adapted OPT language model**.

Trainable parameters: LoRA adapters on OPT (`q_proj`, `v_proj`) — same ~8M params as Model 1. No new layers needed; the concatenation is a zero-parameter operation.

